  "text_md": "|26|||\n|---|---|---|\n|′|σ(i) i|σ(i) i∈J i|\n\nMIGUEL A. CARDONA, DIEGO A. MEJ´IA, AND ANDRES F. URIBE-ZAPATA ´\n\n′ ( ∧ ) ′ σ(i) = 1 for some i ∈ J, then Ξ₁(b′) = Ξ₁(∼bi′) = 0, so Ξ g(σ) ∧ b = 0. In\n\nconclusion, Ξ(a) = Ξ₀(g(σ⁰)), where σ⁰ is the constant map onto 0.\n\n3.15\nWe wonder whether the results of this section are valid when restricting to fams with the uap. These are not in general. For example, let a ⊆ ω be the set of even numbers, B := P (ω), let B₀ be the field of sets over ω generated by a and let F ⊆ P (ω) be the filter of subsets of ω containing {0, 1}. Let Ξ₀ be the (unique) probability measure on B such that Ξ(a) = 3 2 (which is clearly uniformly supported). By Theorem 3.15, there is a unique probability fam Ξ on 〈B₀ ∪ F 〉 extending Ξ₀ such that Ξ(b) = 1 for all b ∈ F, in particular, Ξ({0, 1}) = 1. Then, Ξ({0}) = Ξ(a ∩ {0, 1}) = Ξ₀(a) = 2 3 and, naturally, Ξ({1}) = 1 3. However, Ξ cannot have the uap because no singleton can have measure 3 2 under a probability fam with the uap (by Theorem 2.18). This also shows that Theorem 3.8 is not valid in general when restricting to the uap: both Ξ₀ and ΞF are uniformly supported, but any common extension extends Ξ and thus cannot have the uap.\n\nWe can validate Theorem 3.15 to fams with the uap under further conditions.\n\nTheorem 3.16. In Theorem 3.15, assume that B = P (X) and that B₀ contains all the finite subsets of X. If Ξ₀ has the uap, then so does Ξ. Likewise for “uniformly supported”.\n\nProof. If all finite sets have Ξ₀-measure zero, then the same is true for Ξ. So assume that Ξ₀ is uniformly supported with support P. Since B₀ contains all finite sets, all finite sets in P with positive measure must be singletons (by minimality). It is not hard to show that P is a support of Ξ, so Ξ is uniformly supported.\n\n3.16\nSimilarly, we can validate the extension results when adding one set to the subalgebra.\n\nLemma 3.17. In the context of Lemma 3.11, assume that B = P (X) and that C contains all the singletons. Then, if Ξ has the uap, then Ξ ′ can be found with the uap. As a consequence, Ξ can be extended to a fam into K with the uap on the whole P (X).\n\nHere, “uap” can be replaced by “uniformly supported”, even more, extensions of Ξ can be found with the same support.\n\nProof. The result is trivial when all singletons have Ξ-measure zero. So assume that Ξ is uniformly supported. The argument in the proof of Lemma 3.11, when replacing A by a support of Ξ, allows to find a uniformly supported Ξ ′ with the same support as Ξ. The rest follows by Zorn’s Lemma.\n\n3.17\nWe ask whether (iii) ⇒ (i) of Theorem 3.8 is valid when Ξ₀ and Ξ₁ have the uap for finding Ξ with the uap, under the condition that both B₀ and B₁ contain all singletons. If, say, Ξ₀ is free then Ξ₁ and Ξ must be free, but we do not know how to obtain an uniformly supported Ξ when Ξ₀ and Ξ₁ are uniformly supported.\n\nQuestion 3.18. Assume that B₀ and B₁ are fields of sets over X, containing all the singletons, and Ξ₀ and Ξ₁ are uniformly supported fams with the uap on B₀ and B₁, respectively. Assume that Ξ₀ and Ξ₁ are compatible (in the sense of Theorem 3.8 (iii)). Is there a uniformly supported fam that extends both Ξ₀ and Ξ₁?\n\nA strategy to solve this problem may suggest a short proof of Theorem 3.8.\n",
